@startuml
title Protocol Emulator ASIC — planned architecture
top to bottom direction
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

package "Demo host / board" {
  component "Linux PC\nhost GUI built; board acceptance open" as pc
  component "Tiny Tapeout demo board\nRP2040 controller (Raspberry Pi Pico)" as board
  component "Protocol wires" as wires
}

package "Tiny Tapeout top" {
  component "Passive SPI loader\npe_ctrl" as loader
  package "Loader readback A1 — landed 2026-09-24 (R1 framed bus supersedes the raw echo)" {
    component "Host response bus\nuio[6] MISO frame; commit-latched LOAD echo\nR1: uio[4:7] CS_N/MOSI/MISO/SCK" as readback
  }
  package "Programmable SoC — pe_soc" {
    component "CPU\nfirmware engine" as cpu
    component "Instruction SRAM\n1024 × 16" as imem
    component "CPU data memory\n16-byte register array" as dmem
    component "Frame buffer SRAM\n2 KB" as fbuf
    component "Tick timer\n60 MHz timebase" as tick
    component "Pin matrix\nper-pin OUT / OE / IN / OD" as pinmux

    package "Integrated baseline: 10BASE-T receive" {
      component "DRU\noversampled capture" as dru
      component "Manchester decoder" as manch_rx
      component "Ethernet RX MAC" as eth_rx
      component "CRC-32 check" as crc_rx
    }

    package "Integrated shared word engine" {
      component "Indexed control window\nIO port 0xF; 32 entries; 8-bit data" as window
      component "Programmable timing\nencoded-cell strobe + half-cell level + payload enables" as timing
      component "pe_serdes\n32-bit word engine\ntx_bit_en / rx_bit_en (split)" as serdes
      component "pe_codec_mux TX\nrecommended instance\nNRZI / Manchester / stuffing" as codec_tx
      component "pe_codec_mux RX\nrecommended instance\nNRZI / Manchester / stuffing" as codec_rx
      component "Per-pin level overlay\nbefore OE / open-drain gate" as overlay
      component "TX owner mux\ntx_path: SERDES or eth_tx" as tx_mux
    }

    package "Integrated 10BASE-T TX frame path (landed)" {
      component "Frame source\nfirmware pushes bytes via window" as tx_source
      component "Frame + preamble/SFD\npe_eth_tx engine" as tx_frame
      component "TX-dedicated CRC\nFCS field mode" as tx_crc
      component "IFG / transmit control\n96-cell gap; runt/jabber guard; 8-byte FIFO" as tx_control
    }

  }
  component "Pads\nui_in / uo_out / uio\nuo_out[2] = eth_tx (G6)" as pads
}

pc <--> board : host-control link; transport TBD
board --> loader : SPI program load
loader --> imem : host write port
loader ..> readback : A1 word echo; one frame late
readback ..> pads : MISO on uio[4]
wires --> board : optional SPI MISO readback
board --> pc : optional readback / status
imem --> cpu : instruction fetch
cpu <--> dmem : load / store
cpu <--> tick : timed firmware I/O
cpu <--> pinmux : pin-port reads / writes
pinmux <--> pads : levels, enables, inputs
pads <--> wires : board connections, including optional SPI MISO

wires --> dru : 10BASE-T receive
dru --> manch_rx : recovered half-bits
manch_rx --> eth_rx : decoded bits
eth_rx --> crc_rx : received frame
eth_rx --> fbuf : store-and-forward
fbuf --> cpu : firmware frame window

cpu <--> window : indexed control / data
window --> timing : divisor / mode
window --> serdes : data / lengths / control
timing --> codec_tx : encoded-cell strobe + half_phase
timing --> codec_rx : encoded-cell strobe (plain / NRZI / stuffed)
timing --> serdes : tx_bit_en / rx_bit_en (payload-only)
serdes --> codec_tx : TX bits
codec_tx --> serdes : tx_stuffed (hold)
codec_tx --> overlay : encoded TX level
overlay --> pinmux : override level before OE gate
dru --> codec_rx : rx_wire or Manchester halves
dru --> codec_rx : dru.bit_en (Manchester cell strobe)
codec_rx --> serdes : rx_bit / rx_bit_valid (skip stuff cells)

tx_source --> tx_frame : push bytes
tx_frame --> tx_crc : FCS field
tx_crc --> tx_control
tx_control --> tx_mux : 96-cell IFG / frame TX bit
window --> tx_source : push / TXLEN / TXCTRL / TXSTAT (bank 16-27)
serdes --> tx_mux : tx_bit (default owner)
tx_frame --> tx_mux : eth_tx_bit
tx_mux --> codec_tx : owned tx_bit into shared codec
tx_mux --> pads : uo_out[2] eth_tx (G6, behind pin_oe_bus[7])

package "Host read path (R2) — committed baseline" {
  component "Bounded read port\npe_soc dbg_rd_*" as p_r2port
  component "Wait-word contract\n0xFFFF filler, leading-only skip" as p_r2wait
  p_r2port --> p_r2wait : answer cannot beat the fetch
}

package "R3 debug control — landed 2026-09-25" {
  component "Debug-control contract\n0x21 STEP / 0x22 BP_SET / 0x23 BP_CLR /\n0x24 DEBUG_STATUS (10 words)" as p_r3ctr
  component "bp comparator + debug mux\npe_ctrl / pe_cpu" as p_r3rtl
  component "STOP-BEFORE semantics\n+ the bring-up trap" as p_r3sem
  p_r3ctr --> p_r3rtl
  p_r3ctr --> p_r3sem
}

package "Protocol and timing acts as firmware\none CPU, one pin matrix, TWO tick counters" {
  component "Protocol acts\nI2C adv, SPI m3+CRC, UART RTS/CTS,\nMIDI 31.25k, DMX512-A 250k" as p_proto
  component "Timing acts\nWS2812, servo, DHT11, DS18B20,\nNEC IR, stepper, freqmeter" as p_timing
  component "HC-SR04 ranging\nplanned; the TB is not wired\ninto run_all.sh yet" as p_sr04
  cpu --> p_proto : programs
  cpu --> p_timing : programs
  cpu --> p_sr04 : programs
  pads --> p_proto : pins
  pads --> p_timing : pins
  pads --> p_sr04 : pins
}

package "Chip-host protocol figures and concept pages — the documented wire contract" {
  component "proto-spi-framing\nthe request/response word and bit\nlayout, and the WAIT-WORD rule" as p_docframe
  component "proto-r2-read-path\nthe four read opcodes, the 11-word\nheader, the read-count ceiling" as p_docread
  component "proto-r3-debug-control\nthe four-state machine, STOP-BEFORE,\nthe masked run strap" as p_docdbg
  component "Protocol pages\nhost-chip-protocol\ndebug-control" as p_docproto
  component "Formal pages\nformal-verification\nprotocol-formal-gaps" as p_docformal
  component "Architecture pages\nisa-and-soc\nsoc-wiring-and-memory" as p_docarch
  p_docframe --> p_docread : p_docread --> p_docdbg
  p_docdbg --> p_docproto : p_docproto --> p_docformal : p_docformal --> p_docarch
}

package "Verification — formal campaign" {
  component "SMT / BMC toolchain\nfv_run.sh + smt_induct.sh" as p_fvtool
  component "10 properties / 5 modules\npinmux, eth_tx, pe_ctrl, pe_soc, pe_cpu" as p_fvprops
  component "Inductive IFG floor\neth_tx 96-cell gap, unbounded" as p_fvifg
  component "R3 debug hold\nproved inductively, S1-S4" as p_fvhold
  component "Mutant-kill gate\n14/14 caught, 0 survived" as p_fvmut
  component "THE SMT UNLOCK — the 11th target\nsmt_induct.sh re-runs the same claims\nthrough write_smt2 + yosys-smtbmc/z3,\nwhere a register has ONE next-value\nfunction. It proved the pe_soc C2\nowner-set guard under a second,\nindependent engine." as p_fvsmt
  p_fvtool --> p_fvprops
  p_fvprops --> p_fvifg
  p_fvprops --> p_fvhold
  p_fvmut --> p_fvprops : non-vacuity
  p_fvtool --> p_fvsmt : unlocks
}

package "Delivery model — parallel worktree fleet" {
  component "fw-bus-protocols" as p_wbus
  component "fw-timing-protocols" as p_wtime
  component "host-controller-gui" as p_whost
  component "docs/diag-bus, diag-timing,\ndiag-proto, wiki-features" as p_wdocs
  component "Hardware acceptance\nphysical board — OPEN" as p_whw
  p_whost --> p_wbus : host contract
  p_wbus --> p_wtime : protocol acts
  p_wdocs --> p_wbus : diagrams + wiki depth
  p_wbus --> p_whw : next
}

' LAYOUT ONLY. Nodes at the same depth from a parent fan out horizontally, so
' these sections would land as one wide row and flatten the diagram. The
' hidden chain stacks them instead, which is what the "squarer aspect"
' invariant in diagrams/README.md requires. No architecture is implied.
pads -[hidden]down-> p_r2port
p_r2wait -[hidden]down-> p_r3ctr
p_r3sem -[hidden]down-> p_proto
p_sr04 -[hidden]down-> p_docframe
p_docarch -[hidden]down-> p_fvtool
p_fvsmt -[hidden]down-> p_wbus

note bottom of p_r3ctr
  LANDED 2026-09-25, and this note corrects a first version that said
  otherwise. That version claimed the RTL did not exist, measured in THIS
  worktree -- which is behind main, where the landed control lives (21
  references in pe_ctrl.v, 8 in pe_cpu.v). The lesson is the one this project
  keeps paying for: a local measurement is not a project claim, and the eight
  "missing" persona acts had already been caught the same way in the same
  session.

  THE BRING-UP TRAP is the part a host must design around: once the core is
  HELD, the run strap is ignored BOTH WAYS. A hold is released only by
  DEBUG_BP_CLR or by reset, because
  cpu_exec = (dbg_step === 1'b1) || (run && !(dbg_hold === 1'b1))
  (pe_cpu.v:233). A host that single-steps or hits a breakpoint and then
  re-asserts run=1 to carry on finds the core STILL HELD, with no fault and
  nothing in the response words to explain it.

  THE === FORM IS THE CONTRACT, not decoration. An UNDRIVEN debug input then
  reads as INACTIVE; the plain !dbg_hold form propagates x and freezes the core
  at reset. The convention is enforced where the input is consumed rather than
  at every call site, which is the only place a new call site cannot forget it.
  Leaving those inputs undriven took six acts to their reset after the 5b4731f
  merge.
end note

note bottom of p_proto
  The architectural point of the whole firmware-protocols effort: these are not
  peripherals. They are PROGRAMS on the same CPU, pin matrix and tick, which is
  why a rate the 260-CLOCK UART half-bit tick cannot express (MIDI 31.25 kbaud,
  DMX 250 kbaud) is still reachable -- by counting instructions. Measured on
  the pin: MIDI 31,998.7 ns / 31,251 baud and DMX 3,999.8 ns / 250,010 baud,
  both with zero spread across every frame.

  NAME THE TICK beside any conclusion drawn from it. This SoC has TWO tick
  counters and they are not the same size: port 7 (STATUS) is the UART
  HALF-BIT tick at 260 clocks / 4.33 us, and port 4 (I2CTICK) is a
  free-running 1 us counter built from I2C_TICKS = CLK_HZ / 1_000_000 = 60
  exactly (pe_soc.v:560), so it has no rounding error at all. So "the tick
  cannot express a fraction" is true of the 260-clock tick and FALSE of the
  1 us one, which divides 4.3333 us only inexactly but 4.000 us exactly. The
  acts here count instructions rather than program either tick, so nothing in
  this section depends on which one is meant -- but a reader who is not told
  which tick will carry the wrong conclusion into their own act.
end note

note bottom of p_whw
  THE REMAINING RED. Everything above is simulated and mapped; the physical
  Pico/USB board acceptance is the one item no simulation can close.
end note

note bottom of p_fvsmt
  A COUNT IS AN ASSERTION, so the two numbers in this section are deliberately
  NOT the same kind of thing and must not be conflated:
    * 10 PROPERTIES across 5 modules is what formal/results/summary.txt
      records, and it stays 10. Do not let a larger target count overwrite it.
    * 11 TARGETS is that same 10 plus the SMT unlock below.
  The unlock exists because the built-in `sat -tempinduct` is a self-contained
  k-induction over a sampling netlist in which a register's next-value is
  DUPLICATED per sampling domain, which is why four pe_ctrl claims looked
  toolchain-blocked. write_smt2 + yosys-smtbmc/z3 gives each register ONE
  next-value function. It also validates the pipeline: the known-inductive
  pe_ctrl subset passes there too, so a FAILED run on a hard claim means the
  CLAIM, not the tool.
end note

note bottom of p_docframe
  The three figures are the wire contract drawn, and each is built from the
  RTL rather than from the prose: the response builders, the dbg_state
  equation, the hit latch and the execute gate. proto-spi-framing carries the
  word/bit layout and the WAIT-WORD rule; proto-r2-read-path the 11-word
  header field by field and the read-count ceiling; proto-r3-debug-control
  the state machine, STOP-BEFORE and the masked run strap.

  The six concept pages behind them are wiki/concepts/host-chip-protocol,
  debug-control, formal-verification, protocol-formal-gaps, isa-and-soc and
  soc-wiring-and-memory. The maps name all six so the documentation surface
  is not understated: a page that exists but that no map points at is a page
  the next reader will not find.
end note

note bottom of p_sr04
  HC-SR04 ranging is PLANNED, and is the one firmware act whose testbench is
  not wired into run_all.sh -- so a green suite is currently SILENT about it.
  Measured standalone 2026-09-25: 5 checks fail. See the progress map, which
  records the measurement rather than the intention.
end note

note bottom of pads
  Baseline firmware personas: UART, SPI master, I2C master.
  The passive loader is a separate SPI path used to load instruction SRAM.
  Current aliases: uo_out[0] is UART TX / SPI SCLK; ui_in[0] is UART RX /
  SPI MISO; uio[0:1] are I2C SDA/SCL; uio[2:3] are SPI MOSI/CS_N.
  ui_in[2] is 10BASE-T RX; uo_out[2] is 10BASE-T TX (G6 reclaim of dbg_pc[0]).
end note

note bottom of pads
  The Linux GUI and its PC-to-board transport are planned software; the
  transport and control API are not chosen yet. The RP2040 sits on the Tiny
  Tapeout demo board and is the chip-side SPI host.
end note

note bottom of pads
  Landed 2026-09-24 (manager decision): the R1 framed host bus supersedes the
  A1 raw uio[4] echo — SPI mode 0, MSB-first 16-bit words, 16'hA55A sync,
  CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE, response opcode bit 7, sequence/target echoed; the
  commit-latched word echo lives in the framed LOAD response. Pads: uio[4:7]
  = CS_N/MOSI/MISO/SCK, uo_out[1] = IRQ_N (sticky, active low), ui_in[3:5]
  freed; 19 of 24 committed. See wiki/plans/host-controller-gui.md and
  wiki/plans/pe-ctrl-readback.
end note

note bottom of pads
  Landed 2026-09-24: ONE pe_serdes with split payload-only enables (TX
  advances only on payload cells, serdes.tx_bit_en = tx_codec_cell_en &&
  !tx_stuffed; RX captures only payload cells, serdes.rx_bit_en =
  rx_codec_cell_en && rx_bit_valid). TWO unmodified pe_codec_mux instances are
  clocked per encoded cell -- never per half-cell -- and half_phase is an
  independent 2x half-cell LEVEL; the pad overlay sits before the matrix's
  open-drain gate, and a latched-phase window on port 0xF has
  separate TXLEN/RXLEN. First consumer is the wire loopback (plain and stuffed
  Manchester, word 0x07E0) through TX codec, simulation wire model, DRU/codec
  RX, and SERDES RX; a trailing stuff cell is covered by construction. The
  10BASE-T TX frame consumer (pe_eth_tx) is integrated below and shares the
  TX codec through the tx_path owner mux. Landed 2026-09-25 (plan Tasks 1-5):
  full frame from firmware pushes, 32-entry window, owner mux, G6 uo_out[2]
  pad, loopback acceptance PASS (FCS 9cc5cb34, IFG 102 cells). Plan Tasks 6
  (mutation suites) and 7 (STA + closeout) remain OPEN.
  Low-speed USB and CAN are later personas; JTAG, SWD, and PS/2 are optional.
  Evidence: reviews/2026-09-24/SERDES-INTEGRATION-REVIEW.md.
end note


@enduml
